WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.860
He was the personification of the evil dictator
- clad in his familiar khaki uniform and cap

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:10.219
and chomping on his cigar, he ruled with an
iron fist over his island nation.

00:00:10.219 --> 00:00:15.099
He took on the United States and brought the
world to the brink of nuclear holocaust.

00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:20.250
Shifting attention to his own country he decimated
the economy, creating a Communistic dictatorship

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.340
that forced the people to revere him, even
as he ruined their lives.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:32.680
In this week’s Biogragphics we take a close
look at the life of Fidel Castro.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:45.760
Birth of a Dictator

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:51.250
Fidel Castro was born on August 13th, 1926
in the small farming village of Manacas in

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.190
northern Oriente, Cuba.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:58.410
His father,Angel Castro y Igas, had immigrated
from Spain in 1898 and picked up work as a

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:03.020
bricklayer and railroad worker before starting
his own business selling lemonade to field

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:04.020
workers.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:11.040
From this humble start, he built a small sugarcane
empire comprising 26,000 acres and 300 worker

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.040
families.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.640
Angel’s first wife, Maria Argota, gave birth
to two children and then either died or simply

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:17.869
walked out on the family.

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:22.870
Angel soon took up with his young maid, Lina
Ruz Gonzalez, who bore him three children;

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:24.940
Angela, Ramon and Fidel.

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:27.860
The couple were married shortly after Fidel’s
birth.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:32.570
They were a relatively wealthy family who
lived in a Galician style two storey country

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:33.570
house.

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.290
Still, they lived a decidedly country lifestyle
with chickens and pigs wandering through the

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:38.290
house.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:41.640
Fidel was named after a local politician,
with his name meaning ‘Faithful’.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.610
He had his father’s temperament, which meant
that he was prone to violent outbursts.

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:49.450
His sister recalls that if he was playing
baseball and his team were losing he would

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.560
simply gather up the equipment and walk off
the field.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:56.970
Close friend and future novelist Gabriel Garcia
Marquez recalled that Fidel was the worst

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.939
loser he had ever met.

00:01:58.939 --> 00:02:03.060
When he reached school age, Fidel was sent
to study in Santiago, the second largest city

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.060
in Cuba.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.460
For the first two years he stayed with his
Godparents and as homeschooled by them.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:11.239
After that he was enrolled at the Marist La
Salle School along with brothers Ramon and

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:12.239
Raul.

00:02:12.239 --> 00:02:15.569
Fidel soon gained a reputation as a troublemaker
and a bully.

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:18.370
When he was in the fourth grade, Fidel’s
father received a letter stating that his

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:21.560
three boys were the biggest bullies in the
entire school.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.580
Angel was furious and pulled the boys out
of the school.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:27.860
This enraged Fidel who threatened to burn
down the house.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:32.580
He ended up being set to a more demanding
Jesuit school in Havana, with his father threatening

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.800
to cut off his allowance if his grades dropped
below a certain level.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.430
Fidel responded by forging his school reports.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:43.950
Castro proved to be a decidedly average student
but there was one area in which he excelled.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:48.239
He had a photographic memory, a fact which
hugely impressed his fellow students.

00:02:48.239 --> 00:02:51.620
They would call to him a page number from
their textbooks and he would recite the page

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:52.800
word for word.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.200
In his teens, Fidel began to develop his ability
as a public speaker and debater.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.690
After an initial rejection he gained acceptance
into the Allevenada Literary Academy, which

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:03.830
was the literary wing at the Jesuit school.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:09.069
At first he was paralyzed by stage fright
but he gradually became more relaxed and confident

00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:10.230
in his presentations.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:14.569
In October, 1945, Fidel entered law school
at the University of Havana.

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:18.930
At the time the university was a self governing
body which forbade the police or army from

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:20.470
setting foot on campus.

00:03:20.470 --> 00:03:24.890
As a result, it was a hotbed of clandestine
activity by gangsters and political agitators.

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:31.470
It was a very attractive environment for the
hotheaded Castro.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:36.870
Political Agitator

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:44.650
Fidel’s first foray into political agitation
was precipitated by a rise in bus fares that

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:48.459
had been authorized by Cuban President Ramon
Grau.

00:03:48.459 --> 00:03:52.920
Castro organized a protest against the fare
rise and led a march to the Presidential palace.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:56.769
The police beat the students, with Fidel himself
receiving slight injuries.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:04:01.370
He used the incident to his advantage by going
to the press and receiving some sympathetic

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:02.370
coverage.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:06.489
At the time that Castro enrolled at the University,
there were two main gangster groups who were

00:04:06.489 --> 00:04:11.970
vying for control; the Socialist Revolutionary
Movement (MSR) and the Insurrectional Revolutionary

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:13.099
Union (UIR).

00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:16.449
There were frequent violent clashes between
the two groups.

00:04:16.449 --> 00:04:20.129
Fidel quickly sized up the situation and began
to maneuver between the two groups.

00:04:20.129 --> 00:04:25.659
In December, 1946 there was an assassination
attempt on a leading member of the UIR and

00:04:25.659 --> 00:04:30.050
Castro was fingered as the triggerman, his
assumed motive being to ingratiate himself

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:31.680
with MSR leadership.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.340
As it turned out, it was the leader of UIR,
Emilio Tro, who took Castro under his wing.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:40.680
He gave Fidel a pistol, which the young budding
revolutionary carried with him at all times.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:44.659
Tro had been planning an invasion of the Dominican
Republic in conjunction with a group of Dominican

00:04:44.659 --> 00:04:49.180
exiles in response to the terrible conditions
brought about by the rule of Rafael Trujillo.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:53.240
Castro quickly jumped on board and sailed
with about 12,000 fellow revolutionaries to

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:56.589
Cayo Confites on July 29th, 1947.

00:04:56.589 --> 00:05:00.830
For two months they underwent paramilitary
training before setting off for the Dominican

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:01.830
mainland.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.289
Meanwhile,Trujillo had learned of the planned
attack and had even appealed to the United

00:05:05.289 --> 00:05:06.349
States for aid.

00:05:06.349 --> 00:05:10.659
The revolutionary leaders got cold feet and
called off the invasion and the embarrassed

00:05:10.659 --> 00:05:15.360
Cuban army began rounding up the ships and
taking the would-be attackers into custody.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.569
Castro manage to evade custody by jumping
ship and swimming the eight miles to shore.

00:05:20.569 --> 00:05:25.680
In 1947, Castro visited a new model prison
on the Isle of Pines as part of his legal

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:26.680
studies.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:31.169
When he returned to Havana he criticized the
prison and its inhumane treatment of its inhabitants.

00:05:31.169 --> 00:05:35.520
The following year he was part of a student
congress that travelled to Bogota, Colombia.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:40.219
On April 7th, the group met with the leader
of the Liberal Party, Jorge Gaitan, who looked

00:05:40.219 --> 00:05:41.969
likely to win the upcoming general election.

00:05:41.969 --> 00:05:44.750
Two days later, Gaitan was shot dead.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:49.039
Bogota erupted in violence and the students
from Cuba were caught in the middle of it.

00:05:49.039 --> 00:05:53.430
Castro became actively involved, participating
in the takeover of a police station.

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:59.719
After three days, the authorities restod order,
but not before some 3,500 people had been

00:05:59.719 --> 00:06:00.719
killed.

00:06:00.719 --> 00:06:04.370
Castro returned to Havana where he became
a follower of Senator Eddie Chibas, the main

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:06.039
opponent of President Grau.

00:06:06.039 --> 00:06:08.830
Chibas was the founder of the Cuban People’s
Party.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:12.870
Castro campaigned hard for Chibas in his unsuccessful
bid for the presidency in 1948.

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:14.979
Still, the two men were never close.

00:06:14.979 --> 00:06:19.770
Chibas saw Fidel as somewhat of an embarrassment
due to his gangster background and Fidel only

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:25.979
ever viewed the leader as an obstacle to his
own path to power.

00:06:25.979 --> 00:06:32.960
Budding Politician

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:37.949
In order to gain political respectability,
Fidel cut off association with his gangster

00:06:37.949 --> 00:06:38.949
associates.

00:06:38.949 --> 00:06:43.069
He gave speeches on campus denouncing the
two main gangs and naming names of those who

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:45.080
were responsible for criminal activity.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:48.929
This made him a target for both groups and
he was forced into hiding.

00:06:48.929 --> 00:06:54.009
Around this time Fidel, who was generally
awkward and shy around women, met Mirta Diaz-Balart,

00:06:54.009 --> 00:06:56.209
the daughter of one of the wealthiest men
in Cuba.

00:06:56.209 --> 00:07:01.969
The couple fell in love and were married in
1948, with Mirta’s father gifting them $10,000

00:07:01.969 --> 00:07:04.050
for ap three month honeymoon in the United
States.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:08.349
Most of their time was sent in New York, where
Fidel learned some English.

00:07:08.349 --> 00:07:12.219
Returning to Cuba, the newlyweds moved into
a hotel across the street from a military

00:07:12.219 --> 00:07:13.219
camp in Havana.

00:07:13.219 --> 00:07:18.930
In September, 1949 Mirta gave birth to a son,
who would come to be known as Fidelito.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:22.080
Having graduated from University, Fidel began
a small law practice.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:25.759
Most of his time, however, was spent dabbling
in politics.

00:07:25.759 --> 00:07:31.469
On August 15th, 1951, People’s Party leader
Eddie Chibas was giving his usual Sunday radio

00:07:31.469 --> 00:07:35.589
address, urging the people to stop accepting
the corruption of the government and take

00:07:35.589 --> 00:07:36.589
action.

00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:39.339
He then pulled out a pistol and shot himself
in the stomach.

00:07:39.339 --> 00:07:43.949
The dramatic act was intended to rouse the
people but Chibas had spoken for too long

00:07:43.949 --> 00:07:46.249
and his suicide attempt was not broadcast.

00:07:46.249 --> 00:07:50.559
For the next eleven days, Chibas lay in a
hospital bed in terrible agony.

00:07:50.559 --> 00:07:54.030
At his side the whole time was Fidel Castro.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:58.449
When the leader died, Castro was prominent
as part of the honor guard that stood outside

00:07:58.449 --> 00:08:01.779
the University Hall of Honor, where Chibas
lay in state.

00:08:01.779 --> 00:08:05.849
As the military escort prepared to lead the
body through the streets in a large procession,

00:08:05.849 --> 00:08:09.479
Castro entreated the captain to divert the
route to the Presidential Palace.

00:08:09.479 --> 00:08:13.529
Fidel was convinced that he could rouse the
people to overthrow the government.

00:08:13.529 --> 00:08:17.919
But the army captain refused, fearing that
a bloodbath would ensue.

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:21.639
The following year, Castro ran for a seat
on the Chamber of Deputies.

00:08:21.639 --> 00:08:26.310
He managed to garner the support of the majority
of the People’s Party members and looked

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:28.009
in line to win in the upcoming election.

00:08:28.009 --> 00:08:33.349
However, on March 10th, former president Fulgencio
Batista staged a military coup and seized

00:08:33.349 --> 00:08:34.349
power.

00:08:34.349 --> 00:08:38.329
He began rounding up political adversaries,
including members of the People’s Party.

00:08:38.329 --> 00:08:40.740
Fidel and his brother Raoul went into hiding.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:45.110
The Batista coup diverted Castro from what
could have been a promising diplomatic career

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:48.310
in favor of becoming a fully fledged revolutionary.

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:51.630
He gathered together his own political group,
drawn from former People’s Party members

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.980
and followers from his University days.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:57.540
Traveling up and down the country, he put
his oratorical and propaganda skills to full

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:58.540
effect.

00:08:58.540 --> 00:09:06.640
After fourteen months, he had a following
of 12,000 people.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:12.300
Rebel Leader

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:15.120
Castro ran his organization with military
precision and discipline.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.139
Drinking alcohol was forbidden and he imposed
strict sexual standards.

00:09:19.139 --> 00:09:24.170
He also organized his forces at a cellular
level, with members of each cell being unaware

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:26.050
of the existence of other cells.

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:31.040
By the middle of 1953, Castro had the support
base he needed to stage his own coup.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:34.780
What he didn’t have were weapons - or the
money to buy them.

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:37.970
His solution was to attack a military base
and seize its weapons.

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:41.160
The base selected was located at Moncado.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:44.910
The attack, however, was bungled from the
start - one of the twenty six vehicles en

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:48.490
route to the base had a flat tire; another
took a wrong turn and did not arrive until

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:50.240
the attack was well under way.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:53.579
When the remainder of the vehicles arrived
at the base they were surprised to find it

00:09:53.579 --> 00:09:54.579
heavily fortified.

00:09:54.579 --> 00:09:59.019
The military defenders soon beat back the
invaders, with eight of Castro’s men being

00:09:59.019 --> 00:10:00.019
killed.

00:10:00.019 --> 00:10:04.050
The remainder fled, but over the next few
days many of them were rounded up by the authorities.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:07.370
Sixty-nine of them were tortured and then
put to death.

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:10.060
Castro himself was put on trial before a military
tribunal.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:14.460
He used the opportunity to speak out about
the regime of Batista and the harsh treatment

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:15.990
of his fellow revolutionaries.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:19.939
His courtroom defense statement ‘History
Will Absolve Me’ would become probably his

00:10:19.939 --> 00:10:21.250
most famous speech.

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:25.720
Still, he was convicted and sentenced to 15
years imprisonment.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:29.560
Castro and his followers were sent to Isle
of Pines prison.

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:34.310
Fidel used his time there to instill a supreme
level of order and discipline in his men.

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:38.260
The Castro rebels gained a reputation for
their good behavior and were given ever greater

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:39.260
freedoms.

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:45.089
On May 6th, 1954, bowing to public pressure,
president Batista granted amnesty to Castro

00:10:45.089 --> 00:10:46.089
and his men.

00:10:46.089 --> 00:10:49.560
The show of good faith from the president,
however, did not stop Castro from speaking

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:50.730
about the regime.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:55.060
When two of his men were badly beaten by Batista
enforcers, Fidel became increasingly concerned

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:56.220
for his own safety.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:11:00.199
He would move houses every two to three days
and then, just two months after being released

00:11:00.199 --> 00:11:03.040
from prison, he left Cuba for Mexico.

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:07.759
It was Castro's plan to build up and train
a new wing of his revolutionary army in Mexico

00:11:07.759 --> 00:11:12.709
and then return to join forces with those
already in Havana in a huge push to oust the

00:11:12.709 --> 00:11:13.879
Batista regime.

00:11:13.879 --> 00:11:18.319
His Mexican army began as a group of about
sixty Cuban defectors who lived in six small

00:11:18.319 --> 00:11:22.150
houses under strict disciplinary conditions
imposed by Fidel.

00:11:22.150 --> 00:11:26.519
It was during this period that Castro met
Ernesto ‘Che’ Guevara, who would play

00:11:26.519 --> 00:11:28.930
a key role on the coming revolution.

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:34.080
Castro launched his revolution in late November,
1956 from the port of Tuxpan on the Mexican

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:35.080
coast.

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:39.410
It was planned to coincide with another uprising
organized by a rival anti-Batista group.

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:44.940
Fidel had bought a 60 foot boat that was designed
to take a maximum of 25 passengers and piled

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:47.120
82 of his men onboard.

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:51.490
As it chugged its way toward Cub, the boat
was in constant danger of capsizing.

00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:56.329
The journey was so slow that the planned five
day trip took seven days, meaning that they

00:11:56.329 --> 00:11:58.410
would miss the uprising of the other group.

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:01.769
The boat beached at a place called Purgatory
Point on December 2nd.

00:12:01.769 --> 00:12:05.290
But by now Batista’s army, who had already
put down the first rebellion, had learned

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:10.050
that Castro was on his way and had planes
overhead ready to strafe the invaders and

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:11.300
bomb the area.

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:14.550
Only about a dozen men survived, Castro and
Guevara among them.

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:18.709
They began a three day march through the forest,
constantly being hounded from the air by the

00:12:18.709 --> 00:12:19.829
Cuban air force.

00:12:19.829 --> 00:12:23.690
They finally reached the Sierra Maestra region
where they settled with the local villagers

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:25.600
and began to regroup.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:30.209
Six weeks later Castro led thirty three men
on a guerilla raid on a tiny army garrison

00:12:30.209 --> 00:12:31.339
in La Plata.

00:12:31.339 --> 00:12:34.759
Two soldiers were killed and the rebels stole
off with a number of weapons.

00:12:34.759 --> 00:12:38.720
A month after this attack, Castro decided
it was time to get his propaganda machine

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:39.720
in motion.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:42.990
He sent one of his men to Havana to bring
a foreign journalist back to the camp.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:46.720
The man chosen was from the New York Times
and soon a three part story had appeared in

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:51.180
that paper describing the massive military
buildup that Castro was organizing in the

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:52.560
south of Cuba.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:56.209
Castro's support base grew exponentially,
with more and more disaffected Cubans joining

00:12:56.209 --> 00:12:57.209
his ranks.

00:12:57.209 --> 00:13:02.230
In March, 1958, younger brother Raoul began
to establish a second force out of Oriente,

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:03.560
which soon grew in number.

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:08.050
Raoul proved to be a highly capable military
commander, with his force capturing and destroying

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:10.579
many planes, tanks and military vehicles.

00:13:10.579 --> 00:13:14.570
In April, Fidel called upon the people of
Cuba to stage a nationwide strike.

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:17.850
He believed that this would be the start of
his long planned for uprising.

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:21.699
President Bautista responded by threatening
to shoot anyone who participated in the strike.

00:13:21.699 --> 00:13:25.890
As a result, more than 140 people were shot
down in the street.

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:31.300
But this action only fuelled Castro’s support
base and even more people streamed to him.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:34.889
With the tide now decidedly turned in his
favor, Castro decided to move.

00:13:34.889 --> 00:13:40.300
He sent Che Guevara and another of his commanders,
Camilo Cienfuegos, on a westward march toward

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:42.320
Havana, along with their rebel forces.

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:46.160
The government forces put up virtually no
resistance and the rebels were able to take

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:48.370
control, one town after another.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:53.499
On December 31st, 1958, with Castro’s men
closing in, Batista resigned and fled the

00:13:53.499 --> 00:13:54.499
country.

00:13:54.499 --> 00:13:57.660
Three days later, Castro arrived in Santiago
to a hero’s welcome.

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:00.449
He gave a speech in front of 200,000 people.

00:14:00.449 --> 00:14:05.379
Hours later, Guevara and Cienfuegos marched
into Havana and seized control of the reigns

00:14:05.379 --> 00:14:07.240
of power.

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:11.149
The Castro era had begun.

00:14:11.149 --> 00:14:16.560
Castro’s Cuba

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:21.939
The transfer of power was amazingly orderly.

00:14:21.939 --> 00:14:24.459
Castro had wanted his men against looting
or destroying property.

00:14:24.459 --> 00:14:29.010
He had come to power on the backs of a huge
army of poor and illiterate peasants.

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:32.689
Now that he had gained power, he would need
educated men to help him to govern.

00:14:32.689 --> 00:14:37.660
He chose to appoint his former professor,
Jose Miro Cardona, as prime minister and Manuel

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:39.370
Urrutia to be president.

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:41.910
He made himself supreme commander of armed
forces.

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:45.930
Behind the scenes, however, Castro put the
organization in place to ensure that he was

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:47.870
the holder of overall power.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:52.199
In the weeks following the revolution more
than 500 Batista officials were tried, convicted

00:14:52.199 --> 00:14:53.370
and put to death.

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:57.970
That number would rise to 1,900 over the coming
year.

00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:02.540
Within 7 months of seizing power, Castro decided
that he no longer had any need for the puppet

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:03.540
government.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:07.120
On July 16th, he announced that he was resigning
his position as army commander because he

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:11.480
could no longer work with President Urrutia,
who he accused of corruption.

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:13.790
Castro then disappeared for a number of days.

00:15:13.790 --> 00:15:17.649
Just as Fidel had intended, the people rose
up in protest and demanded that the President

00:15:17.649 --> 00:15:18.649
must go.

00:15:18.649 --> 00:15:22.750
A frightened Urrutia sought sanctuary in the
Venezuelan embassy before escaping the country.

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:27.329
This allowed Castro to return in triumph and
appoint himself as President.

00:15:27.329 --> 00:15:29.740
Castro now imposed totalitarian rule.

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:33.959
Those who did not give him their absolute
support were in danger of being seized and

00:15:33.959 --> 00:15:36.959
put on trial or simply shot.

00:15:36.959 --> 00:15:41.480
Within a few short months all signs of resistance
within Cuba, including critical media, had

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:42.480
been removed.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:46.369
Now Castro fixed his attention on securing
external security.

00:15:46.369 --> 00:15:50.680
A focus was put on increasing the size and
strength of the army.

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:54.660
Six months after seizing power, Castro visited
the United States, where he was greeted with

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:58.499
enthusiasm by the media, politicians and public
alike.

00:15:58.499 --> 00:16:05.199
Behind the scenes, however, the Eisenhower
government were already making plans for Castro’s

00:16:05.199 --> 00:16:06.779
removal.

00:16:06.779 --> 00:16:09.949
International Tensions

00:16:09.949 --> 00:16:17.990
In February 1960, the Cuban leader signed
an agreement with the Russians, by which Cuban

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:20.820
sugar would be traded for military arms and
oil.

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:25.209
This was at the height of the Cold War and
many of the oil refineries in Cuba were American

00:16:25.209 --> 00:16:26.209
owned.

00:16:26.209 --> 00:16:30.279
When the owners refused to process Soviet
oil imports, Castro seized their businesses.

00:16:30.279 --> 00:16:35.040
The US Government responded by cutting 700,000
tons from the US annual purchase commitment.

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:39.199
In turn, Castro nationalized US owned agricultural
and industrial businesses.

00:16:39.199 --> 00:16:43.510
Castro's’ move had a huge impact on the
US Mafia, which lost about $100 million worth

00:16:43.510 --> 00:16:45.970
of property in the tourist industry alone.

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:50.420
During the period of the nationalization of
industry, more than 200,000 professional and

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:52.360
upper class citizens fled the country.

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:56.300
Relations between the United States and Cuban
governments became so strained that, in March

00:16:56.300 --> 00:17:01.339
1960, President Eisenhower green lighted the
training of a group of Cuban exiles to stage

00:17:01.339 --> 00:17:03.559
an invasion to seek control.

00:17:03.559 --> 00:17:09.209
The ball was picked up by President Kennedy,
resulting in the disastrous Bay of Pigs invasion.

00:17:09.209 --> 00:17:13.069
Castro had gotten wind of the invasion and
had his forces ready and waiting.

00:17:13.069 --> 00:17:17.500
With the lack of promised US air support,
the Cuban rebels were cut to pieces.

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:23.720
In December 1st, 1961 Castro went on national
television and declared that he was a Marxist-Leninist

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:25.929
and would be one until the day he died.

00:17:25.929 --> 00:17:30.520
The Soviets took a wary view of their newly
declared Communist fellow but the Americans

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:34.930
responded immediately, imposing a total economic
blockade of Cuba.

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:39.100
This led to economic disaster, with massive
food shortages resulting.

00:17:39.100 --> 00:17:41.950
Finally, the Soviet Government decided to
embrace Castro.

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:44.490
They offered to station Soviet missiles on
the island.

00:17:44.490 --> 00:17:49.360
In mid October, 1962 the American government
got concrete evidence of the missile operation,

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:52.570
leading to what became known as the Cuban
Missile Crisis.

00:17:52.570 --> 00:17:56.420
President Kennedy placed a total military
blockade around Cuba and announced that a

00:17:56.420 --> 00:18:01.940
military attack from Cuba on any nation would
be seen as a direct attack by the Soviet Union

00:18:01.940 --> 00:18:03.640
on the United States.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:07.380
Castro urged the Russians to make a pre-emptive
strike on the United States if the Americans

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:08.730
attacked his island.

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:13.340
After thirteen days, during the world was
a hair trigger away from Nuclear destruction,

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:16.789
the Russians backed down and agreed to remove
the missiles.

00:18:16.789 --> 00:18:20.970
Castro was left completely out of the negotiation
process - and he was not happy.

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:25.279
Soviet President Nikita Khrushchev tried to
placate him by inviting Castro on a 40-day

00:18:25.279 --> 00:18:26.649
trip through the Soviet Union.

00:18:26.649 --> 00:18:31.260
Durg the trip, Khrushchev offered to provide
economic aid to the struggling island.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:35.650
In 1965 the United States announced that it
was willing to take in Cuban refugees.

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:40.820
This led to a mass exodus with a resultant
huge loss of skills and manpower.

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:51.860
The Sugar Cane Fiasco

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:56.580
With the international situation having settled,
Castro set his focus on making agricultural

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:58.570
reforms to improve the economy.

00:18:58.570 --> 00:19:03.580
He wanted to increase the sugar harvest and
set a target for national production in 1965

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:07.919
of 5.5 million tons, increasing to 10 million
tons by 1970.

00:19:07.919 --> 00:19:11.640
These expectations, however, were totally
unreasonable.

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:16.120
Still he poured nearly all of the country’s
economic resources into the sugarcane industry,

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:18.820
leaving all other economic activity barren.

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:22.690
The whole country, including the elderly,
young children and military workers were put

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:26.600
to work in the fields in a desperate quest
to reach his targets.

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:30.650
Even Castro himself was said to have cut cane
for four hours every day.

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:36.039
As the rest of the Cuban economy fell by between
20 and 40%, the sugarcane project failed dismally

00:19:36.039 --> 00:19:37.970
to reach its projected targets.

00:19:37.970 --> 00:19:41.120
In 1970, Castro announced that the plan had
failed.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:44.529
Typical of his flair for the dramatic he resigned
his presidency.

00:19:44.529 --> 00:19:49.289
The people demanded he return and soon he
was back in power with the sugar cane crisis

00:19:49.289 --> 00:19:50.289
behind him.

00:19:50.289 --> 00:19:54.940
Throughout the 1970’s Castro began looking
for ways to extend his influence abroad.

00:19:54.940 --> 00:19:58.950
He began involving himself in revolutionary
movements and sending forces to train and

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:00.510
lead various guerrilla operations.

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:06.360
His support of civil wars in Angola and Somalia
ate up more of his country's limited resources.

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:10.470
During the early 1980’s sugar prices and
production had increased significantly.

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:14.340
This allowed the Cuban economy to grow at
a rate of 24%.

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:19.250
But when the sugar price slumped dramatically,
ballooning Cuba’s international debt to

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:20.250
the west to $6 billion.

00:20:20.250 --> 00:20:24.029
At the same time, she owed $19 billion to
the Soviet Union.

00:20:24.029 --> 00:20:28.530
The Soviets tried to help by increasing trade
but, with the fall of the Soviet Union in

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:30.620
1989, all the agreements fell apart.

00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:33.950
The already weak Cuban economy was severely
restricted.

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:39.029
Strict rationing was put in place, the electricity
supply became sporadic and tractors were replaced

00:20:39.029 --> 00:20:40.820
with ox-drawn carts.

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:45.690
With his country on the brink of collapse,
in 1993 Castro allowed Cubans to start private

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:46.690
businesses.

00:20:46.690 --> 00:20:50.080
The following year he permitted foreign investors
to own Cuban businesses.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:56.790
The economy, which had declined by 40% in
the past three years, finally began to grow.

00:20:56.790 --> 00:21:05.790
The Death of a Dictator

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:10.669
By the 1990’s Castro was an aging, isolated
and largely depressed dictator.

00:21:10.669 --> 00:21:14.309
The majority of his close associates were
either dead or had been exiled.

00:21:14.309 --> 00:21:17.929
He still kept up his habit of only staying
in a place for two or three nights, so he

00:21:17.929 --> 00:21:21.779
was constantly being moved around in a bulletproof
Mercedes limousine.

00:21:21.779 --> 00:21:25.269
During this period Castro’s tight grip on
the country seemed to loosen slightly.

00:21:25.269 --> 00:21:30.890
But then, in March 2003, he imposed a crack
down which saw scores of journalists, students

00:21:30.890 --> 00:21:34.169
and professional people arrested as dissidents
of the regime.

00:21:34.169 --> 00:21:38.679
In July 2006, the eighty-year-old Castro underwent
surgery for internal bleeding.

00:21:38.679 --> 00:21:42.850
The following year he handed over the Presidential
reigns to his brother Raul.

00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:45.399
From then, Fidel’s health gradually deteriorated.

00:21:45.399 --> 00:21:51.539
The end came on November 26, 2016, though
the exact cause of his death was never released.

00:21:51.539 --> 00:22:27.100
The country was in mourning for nine days.

